### What is the distributed rate limiting problem?

A naive rate limiter stores counters in the memory of a single instance. This breaks the moment you run more than one instance.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// WRONG: Per-instance counter. 5 instances = 5x the intended limit.
public class LocalRateLimiter {

    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
    private final int limit = 100;

    public boolean isAllowed(String userId) {
        return count.incrementAndGet() <= limit;
    }
}
```

```text
Problem:
Limit: 100 requests/minute per user
5 gateway instances, each with local counter
Effective limit: 5 x 100 = 500 requests/minute per user
A scripted client easily bypasses the intended limit.
```

### How does Redis solve the problem?

The counter is stored in Redis, keyed by user ID. Every instance reads and updates the same shared counter.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import redis.clients.jedis.Jedis;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class RedisRateLimiter {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(RedisRateLimiter.class.getName());
    private static final int LIMIT = 100;
    private static final int WINDOW_SECONDS = 60;

    private final Jedis jedis;

    public RedisRateLimiter(Jedis jedis) {
        this.jedis = jedis;
    }

    public boolean isAllowed(String userId) {
        String key = "rate:user:" + userId + ":transfers";
        Long current = jedis.incr(key);
        if (current == 1) {
            jedis.expire(key, WINDOW_SECONDS);
        }
        boolean allowed = current <= LIMIT;
        if (!allowed) {
            LOG.warning("Rate limit exceeded for user: " + userId);
        }
        return allowed;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
All 5 gateway instances increment the SAME Redis key.
User SAV2001 sends request 100 -> allowed (count=100)
User SAV2001 sends request 101 -> rejected (count=101, returns 429)
```

### Why must the update be atomic?

A naive read-then-write has a race condition. Two instances can both read the same count and both allow a request.

```lua
-- Atomic Lua script for token bucket in Redis
-- Runs as one indivisible server-side operation
local key = KEYS[1]
local capacity = tonumber(ARGV[1])
local refillRate = tonumber(ARGV[2])
local now = tonumber(ARGV[3])

local data = redis.call('HMGET', key, 'tokens', 'lastRefill')
local tokens = tonumber(data[1]) or capacity
local lastRefill = tonumber(data[2]) or now

-- Refill tokens based on elapsed time
local elapsed = now - lastRefill
local newTokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * refillRate)

if newTokens >= 1 then
    redis.call('HMSET', key, 'tokens', newTokens - 1, 'lastRefill', now)
    redis.call('EXPIRE', key, 120)
    return 1  -- allowed
else
    return 0  -- rejected
end
```

```text
WRONG: Two-step read-then-write
  Instance A reads count=99 -> allows
  Instance B reads count=99 -> allows
  Both write count=100 -> user got 101 requests through

CORRECT: Lua script (atomic)
  Read + check + consume in one Redis operation
  No race condition possible
```

### How do you implement with Bucket4j?

Bucket4j is the standard Java library for token-bucket rate limiting. It integrates with Redis via Redisson for distributed buckets.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import io.github.bucket4j.Bandwidth;
import io.github.bucket4j.Bucket;
import io.github.bucket4j.BucketConfiguration;
import io.github.bucket4j.distributed.proxy.ProxyManager;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class Bucket4jRateLimiter {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(Bucket4jRateLimiter.class.getName());
    private final ProxyManager<String> proxyManager; // Redis-backed via Redisson

    public Bucket4jRateLimiter(ProxyManager<String> proxyManager) {
        this.proxyManager = proxyManager;
    }

    public boolean tryConsume(String userId) {
        Supplier<BucketConfiguration> config = () -> BucketConfiguration.builder()
            .addLimit(Bandwidth.simple(100, Duration.ofMinutes(1))) // 100 req/min
            .build();

        Bucket bucket = proxyManager.builder()
            .build("rate:user:" + userId, config);

        boolean allowed = bucket.tryConsume(1);
        if (!allowed) {
            LOG.warning("Bucket4j: rate limit hit for user " + userId);
        }
        return allowed;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
User SAV2001 calls tryConsume -> true (tokens: 99 remaining)
... 99 more calls -> true (tokens: 0)
101st call -> false (bucket empty, return 429)
After 1 minute: bucket refills to 100 tokens.
```

### How do you enforce at the gateway?

Spring Cloud Gateway has a built-in Redis-backed RequestRateLimiter with a key resolver that extracts the user ID.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.ratelimit.KeyResolver;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Configuration
public class RateLimiterConfig {

    @Bean
    public KeyResolver userKeyResolver() {
        // Extract user ID from JWT claims or API key header
        return exchange -> Mono.just(
            exchange.getRequest().getHeaders()
                .getFirst("X-User-Id"));
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Gateway route with Redis rate limiter
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: transfer-route
          uri: lb://payment-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/transfers/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20
                key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Replenish rate: 10 tokens/second per user
Burst capacity: 20 (allows burst then settles to 10/sec)
Excess: HTTP 429 with Retry-After header
All gateway instances share the same Redis bucket per user.
```

### How do token bucket and leaky bucket compare?

| Aspect | Token Bucket | Leaky Bucket |
|---|---|---|
| Burst behavior | Permits bursts up to capacity | Smooths to constant outflow |
| Implementation | Refill tokens at steady rate | Process at constant rate |
| Use case | Allow bursts (e.g., page load) | Strict smoothing |
| Redis storage | Tokens + last refill timestamp | Queue position |

### What are common mistakes?

The biggest mistake is per-instance counters in a distributed environment.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Per-instance counter. Useless with multiple gateway instances.
public class WrongLimiter {
    private int count = 0;
    public boolean allow() { return ++count <= 100; }
}

// CORRECT: Shared Redis counter with atomic Lua script.
// All instances share one bucket per user in Redis.
// Bucket4j + Redisson or Spring Cloud Gateway RedisRateLimiter.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Return 429 with Retry-After and rate-limit headers so clients back off gracefully. Redis is on the critical path so make it highly available. Layer a per-user limit with a stricter per-endpoint limit for expensive operations. Design a fallback: fail open or drop to a conservative local limit if Redis is briefly unavailable.
